    <body>
      <sec id="article-26086.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Obstructive uropathy represents a structural or functional impediment to urinary flow anywhere along the urinary tract, leading to urine backflow, increased intrarenal pressure, and potential renal impairment. The obstruction may present acutely or chronically, with partial or complete blockage caused by benign prostatic hyperplasia, ureteral calculi, malignancy, or congenital malformations. Progressive obstruction causes hydronephrosis, tubular atrophy, and irreversible nephron loss, ultimately predisposing the patient to chronic kidney disease. Diagnostic evaluation involves urinalysis, measurement of serum creatinine, and imaging modalities such as ultrasonography or computed tomography to assess the level and severity of obstruction. Early identification and prompt urinary tract decompression are critical to prevent renal damage. Timely intervention addressing the underlying cause restores urinary flow, preserves renal function, and minimizes complications associated with prolonged obstruction.</p>
        <p>Participants acquire the ability to recognize the clinical manifestations, laboratory abnormalities, and imaging findings associated with obstructive uropathy, as well as to integrate relevant evidence-based management strategies. The course emphasizes the importance of early intervention to prevent permanent renal injury and reinforces collaboration among clinicians, nurses, radiologists, and pharmacists. Interprofessional teamwork enhances diagnostic accuracy, streamlines decision-making, and ensures safe pharmacologic management in patients with impaired renal function. Nursing staff closely monitor urine output and patient comfort; radiology provides essential imaging interpretation; and pharmacy supports medication optimization. Collaborative practice fosters early recognition of complications, promotes individualized treatment, and reduces the risk of renal failure. The healthcare team achieves superior patient outcomes through improved communication and coordinated assessment, preserves kidney function, and supports long-term education to prevent recurrent urinary tract obstruction.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Apply individualized, patient-centered care plans that incorporate medical, surgical, and supportive strategies to relieve obstruction and restore renal function.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select&#x000a0;the appropriate intervention, such as catheterization, stent placement, or nephrostomy, based on the location and severity of obstruction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate obstructive uropathy from other causes of renal dysfunction by carefully analyzing clinical presentation, urinalysis, and imaging findings.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional team strategies to improve care coordination for patients with obstructive uropathy, aiming to enhance timely identification and optimize patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26086&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26086">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Obstructive uropathy is a urinary tract disorder due to a structural or functional obstruction of urinary flow. Depending on the obstruction's location, backflow of urine into one or both kidneys can cause hydronephrosis, which can be identified by ultrasonography.&#x000a0;Obstructive uropathy can be asymptomatic or present with a wide variety of symptoms, such as a combination of difficulty initiating micturition, acute or chronic urinary retention, or lower abdominal discomfort and distention. The condition can be acute or chronic and affects individuals across all ages and demographics. Obstructive uropathy leads to acute kidney injury in 5% to 10% of all patients with acute renal failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;This condition accounts for up to 55% of all acute kidney injury cases in patients older than 60.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Some cases of obstructive uropathy can be life-threatening, such as obstructive pyelonephritis.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23078">Hydronephrosis and Hydroureter</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Potential causes of obstructive uropathy vary widely. However, the most frequently diagnosed cause in men is benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).&#x000a0;The history and physical examination, including a pelvic examination in women when appropriate, are key in determining the underlying cause.&#x000a0;The diagnosis is typically confirmed with ultrasonography of the bladder and kidneys.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/43410">Ultrasound of the Urinary Tract</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
        <p>Although uncommon, contributing factors and other potential causes include constipation, urethral strictures, posterior urethral valves, phimosis or paraphimosis, prostatic adenocarcinoma, retroperitoneal fibrosis or adenopathy, colorectal endometriosis, ureterocele, urolithiasis, neurogenic bladder dysfunction, parasitic obstructions, bladder endometriosis, and pelvic organ prolapse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Obstructive uropathy can also present in the neonatal period, prompting evaluation for genitourinary tract dilatation and vesicoureteral reflux, and highlighting the importance of intrauterine fetal anatomy ultrasonography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Urinary obstruction affects all age groups but often shows a bimodal distribution, with peaks in infants and older adults. Congenital genitourinary tract abnormalities (eg, posterior urethral valves) typically present in infants and early childhood as bladder outlet obstruction, accounting for approximately 4% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The largest group of affected patients is men older than 60 due to BPH and prostatic malignancy.&#x000a0;Symptoms of urinary retention occur in men with BPH at a rate of 1% to 2% per year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Obstructive uropathy can occur in women, but it is significantly less common.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38803">Female Urinary Retention</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27833">Prostate Cancer</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>When the normal flow of urine through the urinary tract is restricted, back pressure develops, ultimately extending into the collecting system of the kidneys.&#x000a0;Over time, dilation of the urinary tract occurs, and as the renal filtration system becomes affected, obstructive nephropathy develops.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r13">[13]</xref> The mechanism of nephropathy involves multiple factors, including local ischemia from distension and increased intratubular pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;For example, ureteral obstruction from a calculus affects renal hemodynamics, the glomerular filtration rate, and kidney metabolism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Anatomical changes within the renal parenchyma also occur, inducing tubulointerstitial fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref> Ureteral obstruction remains the experimental model for tubulointerstitial renal fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28306">Reflux Nephropathy</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
        <p>In a partial obstruction, angiotensin and angiotensin II type 1 receptors are upregulated, increasing ureteral peristalsis to help relieve the obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Although peristaltic function may be beneficial in a partial obstruction, it is likely to increase distension and intraluminal pressure with complete obstruction. Rat models have demonstrated the influence of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Transforming growth factor&#x000a0;&#x003b2;1 expression was markedly increased in hydronephrotic kidneys,&#x000a0;which may also occur in humans; however, further studies are needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;These factors may lead to irreversible kidney damage, but predicting the time and rate of this occurrence is challenging, highlighting the importance of early identification and treatment of the underlying obstruction.</p>
        <p>The initial renal vascular response to acute outlet obstruction (up to approximately 2 hours) is vasodilatation of the afferent arterioles, with constriction of the efferent vessels, to maintain renal blood flow despite increased intratubular pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;This initial response is mediated by nitric oxide and prostaglandin activity and usually lasts about 90 minutes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;After 3 hours, renal blood flow declines as afferent arterioles constrict in response to elevated thromboxane A2 and angiotensin II, as well as to increased intratubular pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;After 24 hours, afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction becomes clinically significant, reducing renal blood flow by 50% to 60% of normal, and further contributing to renal ischemia and parenchymal injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Postoperative urinary retention is a common complication of surgical procedures, particularly after general or spinal anesthesia in patients with pre-existing urinary conditions such as BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Postoperative urinary retention is typically transient but may become chronic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Long-term severe obstructive uropathy can lead to progressive loss of renal function, kidney failure, decreased number of functioning glomeruli, interstitial fibrosis, cortical thinning, renal atrophy, and bladder dysfunction or atony.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Uncommon causes of obstructive uropathy include ureteroceles, urethral strictures, retrocaval ureter, ureteropelvic junction obstruction, Prune belly syndrome, posterior urethral valves, ureteral carcinoma, urethral stenosis, inguinoscrotal bladder hernia, pelvic endometriosis, extreme uterine prolapse, uterine fibroids, genitourinary tuberculosis, and retroperitoneal fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27478">Postoperative Urinary Retention</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28306">Reflux Nephropathy</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27911">Prune Belly Syndrome</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30820">Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28479">Retroperitoneal Fibrosis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27515">Posterior Urethral Valves</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/155224">Ureterocele</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Obstructive uropathy can lead to progressive and potentially irreversible renal damage. This process involves renal tubular dilation, interstitial inflammation, cortical cyst development, tubulointerstitial fibrosis, acute kidney injury, and glomerular damage&#x02014;including hyalinization, reduced glomerular number, and detached or atubular glomeruli&#x02014;due to increased hydrostatic and intratubular pressures, ischemia, inflammation, and fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Myofibroblast activation, increased extracellular matrix deposition, and interstitial fibrosis eventually replace healthy renal parenchyma with fibrous tissue, resulting in permanent scarring, cortical thinning, and ultimately, renal atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28306">Reflux Nephropathy</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The degree, location, and duration of the obstruction influence the severity of symptoms and complications. Pain is common in acute urinary tract obstructions, but slowly progressive, chronic blockages may be asymptomatic.&#x000a0;In these chronic cases, an elevated serum creatinine level may be the initial presenting sign. Patients may present with abdominal or flank pain when a ureter becomes acutely obstructed, similar to the pain from a ureteral calculus, whereas slowly progressive obstructions may be painless.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," and&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>The location, quality, severity, and modifying factors of pain may help determine the location and underlying cause. For example, dull flank pain with sharp radiation into the lower quadrant or groin may suggest ureterolithiasis as the underlying etiology of obstruction.&#x000a0;Nocturia, dysuria, urinary urgency or frequency, and decreased force of the urinary stream suggest BPH or prostatic adenocarcinoma in men.&#x000a0;Fever&#x000a0;increases the possibility of&#x000a0;concomitant urinary tract infection and sepsis.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27833">Prostate Cancer</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30858">Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal tract symptoms of constipation, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea&#x000a0;suggest fecal impaction, bowel obstruction, or a colonic mass as the cause of&#x000a0;urinary tract obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r48">[48]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostatic malignancy should be considered in the presence of recent unintentional weight loss, night sweats, and hematuria with a hard, nodular prostate on physical examination or an elevated prostate-specific antigen level.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27833">Prostate Cancer</ext-link>"&#x000a0;and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27834">Prostate Cancer Screening</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation therapy and other treatments for prior malignant neoplasms can increase scarring in the genitourinary tract and cause obstructions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r49">[49]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recent surgical procedures, such as appendectomy and hysterectomy, suggest a possible ureteral injury or ligation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30813">Ureteral Injury</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Clinicians must obtain a complete history and perform a thorough review of systems to determine the underlying etiology.&#x000a0;A complete physical examination should focus on the abdomen and genitalia.&#x000a0;A pelvic examination&#x000a0;should be performed in&#x000a0;women when appropriate. The presence of a distended bladder or suprapubic fullness should direct the clinician to the possibility of urinary retention with possible hydronephrosis and obstructive nephropathy.&#x000a0;A digital rectal examination may reveal prostatic enlargement, a hard nodule suggestive of malignancy, or a fecal impaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Neurologic examination, assessing strength, sensation, reflexes, and muscle tone, can be informative.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>A basic metabolic panel should be performed in most patients with evidence of obstructive uropathy, with a specific focus on renal function; an unexpected decrease in renal function should prompt an evaluation for obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;A urinalysis should be performed to exclude a urinary tract infection. A urine protein-to-creatinine ratio and urine electrolytes&#x000a0;may identify renal dysfunction early in the course of the disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A simple bladder scan can easily identify urinary retention.&#x000a0;Bedside ultrasonography can quickly assess the bladder volume for urinary retention and the renal pelves for evidence of hydronephrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;Point-of-care ultrasonography is the most readily available and least invasive test, but it requires skill to perform reliably and interpret correctly at the bedside.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r56">[56]</xref> Comprehensive&#x000a0;ultrasonography imaging is noninvasive, readily available, free of ionizing radiation, can be easily performed for further assessment, and can provide a renal resistive index, which&#x000a0;is useful in diagnosing ureteral obstructions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23078">Hydronephrosis and Hydroureter</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/43410">Ultrasound of the Urinary Tract</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38803">Female Urinary Retention</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27473">Bladder Post Void Residual Volume</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
        <p>Clinicians should be aware that rapid hydration can cause a transient, physiological, nonobstructive hydronephrosis, most pronounced on the right side, which may be visible on ultrasonography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;This physiological, transient hydronephrosis can easily be misdiagnosed as an obstructive abnormality unless a renal resistive index is obtained.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;The renal resistive index is a useful ultrasonographic tool for identifying unilateral ureteral obstruction and differentiating it from nonobstructive hydronephrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;The renal resistive index is determined by the following formula using Doppler ultrasonography as follows:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Renal resistive index = (peak systolic velocity &#x02212; diastolic velocity)/peak systolic velocity&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The normal range is&#x000a0;less than 0.7.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;Resistive index values greater than 0.7 are typically associated with ureteral obstruction but may also be elevated in renal failure or vascular disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r64">[64]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r65">[65]</xref>&#x000a0;The resistive index of both kidneys should be determined because a difference of more than 0.1 is suggestive of unilateral obstructive uropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r66">[66]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r69">[69]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;The renal resistive index is useful for diagnosing ureteral obstruction during pregnancy and for determining whether hydronephrosis is due to an obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r72">[72]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r73">[73]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource,"&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/43410">Ultrasound of the Urinary Tract</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In most clinical situations of obstructive uropathy without urinary retention, the next radiographic test is a computed tomography of the abdomen and pelvis, especially if there is concern for a malignant tumor, retroperitoneal fibrosis, ureteropelvic junction obstruction, ureteral trauma, or ureterolithiasis.&#x000a0;In addition to hydronephrosis and calyceal blunting, pyelorenal and pyelovenous backflow may be visualized on computed tomography imaging.&#x000a0;Additional tests to consider include a retrograde urethrogram, voiding cystourethrogram, cystoscopy with retrograde pyelography, and a renal nuclear scan.&#x000a0;Magnetic resonance imaging can be considered in some situations.&#x000a0;Proximal obstructions tend to cause a greater decline in renal function than distal obstructions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r75">[75]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23078">Hydronephrosis and Hydroureter</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30820">Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28479">Retroperitoneal Fibrosis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Electrolyte abnormalities should be corrected promptly in patients with obstructive nephropathy or uropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;Hospital admission and urgent nephrology consultation may be warranted if renal function is significantly worse from baseline or if there are clinically significant electrolyte abnormalities. A urinalysis should be performed to exclude infection, and antibiotics should be prescribed as indicated. Urine samples&#x000a0;suggestive of infection should be sent for culture.</p>
        <p>Treatment of obstructive uropathy involves promptly addressing the underlying obstruction. Bladder volume measurements can guide further therapy.&#x000a0;Urethral catheterization&#x000a0;should&#x000a0;be attempted, particularly if the obstruction is due to the most frequent etiology, BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r77">[77]</xref>&#x000a0;The initial attempt typically involves placement of a 16- or 18-gauge French Foley catheter. Obstruction may prevent urethral catheterization and may require advanced interventions. A&#x000a0;trial of urethral catheterization with a Coud&#x000e9;-tip Foley catheter could be attempted, but additional interventions may be required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r78">[78]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r79">[79]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r80">[80]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/19020">Difficult Foley Catheterization</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Although rare, suprapubic catheterization may be required when urethral catheterization is not feasible. The length of time the Foley catheter should remain in place is primarily determined by the initial bladder volume drained and the duration of urinary retention, as these factors reflect the degree of bladder muscle damage.&#x000a0;Outpatient follow-up should be arranged, as voiding trials may be necessary after a period of bladder rest and rehabilitation.&#x000a0;Bladder volumes exceeding 1500 mL often require prolonged recovery of bladder muscle function; some individuals may never regain normal micturition.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
        <p>If voiding trials without a catheter are not successful, and maximal medical treatment is administered after a suitable period of bladder rehabilitation and recovery, options include reinsertion of the Foley catheter, a suprapubic catheter, permanent intermittent self-catheterization, or a surgical procedure.&#x000a0;Additional interventions may be indicated, including ureteral stents, drains, or placement of a nephrostomy tube.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;If conservative management is not successful, prostate artery embolization, transurethral resection of the prostate, laser prostatectomy, and Aquablation may be helpful.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;If the detrusor muscle has been permanently damaged and cannot generate sufficient intravesical pressure for emptying, a permanent solution may involve a Foley catheter, a suprapubic tube, or intermittent self-catheterization.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38803">Female Urinary Retention</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26932">Percutaneous Nephrostomy</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Medication therapy may be helpful in certain cases. Medications that inhibit &#x003b1;1-adrenergic receptors, such as tamsulosin and alfuzosin, relax smooth muscle in the bladder neck and prostate, which relieves urinary obstruction symptoms secondary to BPH.&#x000a0;For example, tamsulosin&#x000a0;is beneficial&#x000a0;for moderate-to-severe obstructive symptoms due to BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r82">[82]</xref>&#x000a0;Although orthostasis is a common concern with these medications, studies indicate that &#x003b1;1-blockers specific for BPH, such as tamsulosin and alfuzosin, typically do not cause significant orthostasis in patients with BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r83">[83]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r84">[84]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17401">Alpha-Blockers</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Finasteride and dutasteride are approved by the Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of BPH.&#x000a0;These medications act by inhibiting the 5&#x003b1;-reductase enzyme, blocking the conversion of&#x000a0;testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, which reduces the size of the prostate over time.&#x000a0;However, they typically require 6 to 12 months for maximum clinical effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r85">[85]</xref>&#x000a0;Combination therapy, such as an &#x003b1;1-adrenergic receptor blocker with a 5&#x003b1;-reductase inhibitor, is likely to produce a beneficial synergistic effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r86">[86]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21771">Finasteride</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/16992">5&#x003b1;-Reductase Inhibitors</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/161479">Dutasteride</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Bicalutamide and leuprolide, which act as an antiandrogen and luteinizing hormone agonist, respectively, ultimately reduce serum testosterone to castrate levels and may relieve obstruction over time by shrinking the prostate in patients with prostate cancer.&#x000a0;However, &#x003b1;1-receptor blockers such as alfuzosin and tamsulosin are more effective and act more quickly to relieve obstructive uropathy associated with BPH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r86">[86]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r88">[88]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r88">[88]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17401">Alpha-Blockers</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>In severe cases, a Foley catheter may be required to relieve urinary retention; however, this may result in postobstructive diuresis, which must be recognized and properly managed.&#x000a0;The management of nephropathy due to obstruction begins with relieving the urinary tract obstruction, which can lead to postobstructive diuresis.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38132">Postobstructive Diuresis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28306">Reflux Nephropathy</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/19020">Difficult Foley Catheterization</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38132">Postobstructive Diuresis</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Obstructive pyelonephritis (pyonephrosis) is a potentially life-threatening condition that occurs when&#x000a0;a ureteral obstruction, typically from a calculus, is concurrent with pyelonephritis of the affected kidney.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r90">[90]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Obstructive pyelonephritis is considered a surgical emergency because it rapidly progresses to urosepsis and septic shock without proper treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients generally do not respond to antibiotic treatment alone unless accompanied by immediate drainage of the renal pelvis with a double-J ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy, which should be performed emergently.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30858">Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26932">Percutaneous Nephrostomy</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>A percutaneous nephrostomy is recommended in the more severe cases because it requires less anesthesia, bypasses the obstructed area, and avoids unnecessary manipulation of the affected kidney.&#x000a0;Stent placement may not be possible if the obstruction is severe and cannot be bypassed by a guidewire.&#x000a0;Once the pyelonephritis and infection are well controlled, a definitive procedure can be performed to remove the obstructing calculus.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30858">Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26932">Percutaneous Nephrostomy</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23688">Ureteroscopy</ext-link>" for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis is broad and varies depending on the location, timing, and risk factors. Urinary retention may be acute or chronic and may be secondary to urinary obstruction, infection, medication, adverse effects, neurogenic causes due to spinal cord impingement, or detrusor muscle dysfunction.&#x000a0;Fever or other constitutional symptoms&#x000a0;may indicate infectious etiologies, such as pyelonephritis or spinal epidural abscess. Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29338">Spinal Epidural Abscess</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21222">Epidural Abscess</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Acute, unilateral flank pain in a patient with known renal stone disease may indicate obstruction from ureterolithiasis.&#x000a0;Prior urologic instrumentation should prompt consideration of urethral strictures or clot retention as potential causes of obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r94">[94]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r95">[95]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r96">[96]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r97">[97]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of neurologic deficits should prompt an evaluation for stroke or spinal cord disorders such as cauda equina syndrome or a sacral tumor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r98">[98]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29338">Spinal Epidural Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23776">Ischemic Stroke</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30687">Conus and Cauda Equina Tumors</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>A history of intravenous drug use can suggest an epidural abscess or discitis.&#x000a0;The presence of a malignant tumor raises suspicion for spinal cord or vertebral metastases.&#x000a0;An aortic aneurysm or dissection should be considered as a cause of flank pain in a patient with a history of hypertension and smoking.&#x000a0;Constipation or a fecal impaction can also produce urinary tract obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r99">[99]</xref>&#x000a0;Changes in medication&#x000a0;or recent anesthetic events may lead to urinary retention.&#x000a0;As with all patient presentations, a thorough history and physical examination guide the differential diagnoses and diagnostic evaluation.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27478">Postoperative Urinary Retention</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/21222">Epidural Abscess</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/29345">Spinal Metastasis</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/20603">Aortic Dissection</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis depends on the underlying etiology, duration, and severity of the obstruction. Acute urologic obstruction is more likely to be painful but reversible, and less likely to cause permanent damage to the renal filtration system. In contrast, chronic obstruction is associated with greater long-term adverse effects on kidney function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, proximal obstruction tends to cause a greater decline in renal function than distal obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r75">[75]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>" and&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The development of chronic renal dysfunction is less likely when only one kidney is affected by the initial obstructive process. However, patients with a solitary kidney are generally at higher risk for kidney disease. Persistent or worsening renal dysfunction after resolution of an acute obstruction, though uncommon, may necessitate long-term renal replacement therapy, including hemodialysis or kidney transplantation.&#x000a0;The overall prognosis worsens if concomitant urinary tract infections remain untreated.&#x000a0;Obstructive pyelonephritis can quickly progress to urosepsis if not diagnosed and drained promptly.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30858">Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</ext-link>,"&#x000a0;for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications often arise from the underlying etiology of obstruction, and management varies accordingly; however, any prolonged urinary obstruction can ultimately result in irreversible renal damage and progression to kidney failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r100">[100]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r101">[101]</xref> Early recognition of obstructive signs in fetuses and neonates is critical, as urinary tract obstruction is a leading cause of neonatal renal failure and is associated with significant long-term morbidity and mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;In children with chronic renal failure, obstructive uropathy is&#x000a0;responsible for 16.5% of all pediatric renal transplants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r102">[102]</xref></p>
        <p>Identifying urinary obstruction in utero can improve prognosis, underscoring the importance of anatomic fetal ultrasonography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r103">[103]</xref>&#x000a0;Obstructive uropathy&#x000a0;may increase the likelihood of urinary tract infections and, in some cases, may represent the initial manifestation of obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r104">[104]</xref>&#x000a0;Microscopic hematuria is common following urethral catheterization; however, more significant bleeding requiring intervention, such as continuous bladder irrigation through a 3-way Foley catheter, can occur.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/22710">Gross and Microscopic Hematuria</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>Transient hypotension and bradycardia may occur after placement of the Foley catheter but generally resolve with observation.&#x000a0;Previously, it was thought that the bladder should be drained gradually to avoid hypotension. Results from more recent studies have demonstrated no significant difference in outcomes between rapid and gradual bladder drainage in patients with urinary retention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r105">[105]</xref>&#x000a0;Postobstructive diuresis may occur; these patients should be monitored in the hospital for changes in vital signs and electrolytes.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38132">Postobstructive Diuresis</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Urinary tract obstructions are primarily a urologic condition. The need for additional consultations depends on the underlying cause of the obstruction. These consultations may involve clinicians from various fields, including urology, nephrology, pediatric urology, plastic surgery, oncologic surgery, general surgery, gynecologic oncology, and oncology, as appropriate.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients with obstructive uropathy due to urinary retention should be educated on Foley catheter care or&#x000a0;the proper technique and timing of intermittent self-catheterization, including adequate catheter anchoring and monitoring of urine output. Patients should also receive education on possible alternative therapies and&#x000a0;counseling&#x000a0;on when to seek immediate care,&#x000a0;such as fever, Foley catheter obstruction, gross hematuria, or severe pain. Patients should be counseled on the importance of adhering to recommended surveillance plans to manage underlying conditions and prevent recurrent obstructions. If medications are prescribed,&#x000a0;patients should be informed of potential adverse effects.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Please see StatPearls' companion resources,&#x000a0;"<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/17168">Acute Renal Colic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/18212">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30858">Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38803">Female Urinary Retention</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23078">Hydronephrosis and Hydroureter</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30854">Male Urinary Retention: Acute and Chronic</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25789">Neurogenic Bladder and Neurogenic Lower Urinary Tract Dysfunction</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/26932">Percutaneous Nephrostomy</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27515">Posterior Urethral Valves</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38132">Postobstructive Diuresis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27833">Prostate Cancer</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/27911">Prune Belly Syndrome</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28306">Reflux Nephropathy</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28479">Retroperitoneal Fibrosis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/43410">Ultrasound of the Urinary Tract</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30818">Ureterolithiasis</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30820">Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/489">Urethral Injury</ext-link>," "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30833">Urethral Strictures</ext-link>," and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/31134">Vesicoureteral Reflux</ext-link><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/20410">,</ext-link>" for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Obstructive uropathy can affect individuals of any age or demographic group, and management of the underlying cause often requires coordinated interdisciplinary care. For example, an ovarian mass causing urinary obstruction may necessitate collaboration among urologists, gynecologists, oncologists, and gynecologic oncologists.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r106">[106]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26086.r107">[107]</xref>&#x000a0;Similarly, urinary obstruction resulting from a gastrointestinal tract neoplasm may require the involvement of urology, gastroenterology, oncology, plastic surgery, and general or oncological surgery. Other conditions requiring multidisciplinary management include retroperitoneal fibrosis and posterior urethral valves in neonates.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/95586">Epithelial Ovarian Cancer</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>If the underlying etiology of the obstruction is likely to affect mental health, as may occur with a newly diagnosed malignant tumor, psychological therapy and support should be provided. Close coordination among multiple specialists, nurses, and other members of the healthcare team requires significant effort, but it can improve patient compliance, support patients and their families in coping with a new diagnosis, and ultimately lead to better clinical outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26086.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
